<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suro of Geumgwan Gaya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 42–199 CE (traditional)</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Kim Suro, King Suro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suro is the legendary founder of Geumgwan Gaya, the leading state of the Gaya confederacy in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gimhae">Gimhae</a>. According to tradition he hatched from a golden egg sent from heaven in 42 CE and married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heo-hwang-ok">Heo Hwang-ok</a>, a princess from a distant land. He is honoured as the ancestor of the Gimhae Kim clan, Korea's largest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suro-of-geumgwan-gaya.jpg" alt="Suro of Geumgwan Gaya" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This file is a Work of Kwj2772 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suro_Tomb.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The founding legend of Geumgwan Gaya was recorded in the 13th-century chronicle Garakguk-gi and cited in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-yusa">Samguk yusa</a>. It tells that as the nine chiefs and their people, commanded by heaven, performed ancestral rites with dancing and song, a gold bowl wrapped in red cloth came down from heaven holding six eggs. Twelve days later six princes were born from them. Suro, the firstborn, led the others in setting up six states and claimed the leadership of the Gaya confederacy. His posthumous name was Sureung.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>According to the legend his wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heo-hwang-ok">Heo Hwang-ok</a>, also called Suriratna, was a princess from a distant country named Ayuta, which the story implies lay in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Their children are said to have included King Geodeung, two sons who took the Heo clan name, seven sons who became Buddhist monks, and two daughters, Princess Yeongan and Lady Jijinnaerye. Historians read the legend as a sign of the early belief that kings descended from heaven; several other Korean kingdoms tell of founders born from eggs, among them Jumong of Goguryeo and Park Hyeokgeose of Silla, and the story has been searched for clues to the little-known customs of Gaya.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Suro is said to have died in 199. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munmu-of-silla">Munmu of Silla</a>, counted as his fifteenth-generation descendant, had his spirit tablet honoured with the Silla kings and ordered ancestral rites for him, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-yusa">Samguk yusa</a>. A tomb attributed to him is still maintained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gimhae">Gimhae</a>. The Gimhae Kim, Gimhae Heo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongwon-yi-clan">Incheon Yi clans</a> all trace their descent to him and did not intermarry until the beginning of the 20th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> and Gimhae became sister cities in 2001, and a memorial park to Suro and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heo-hwang-ok">Heo Hwang-ok</a> is being built in Ayodhya by the South Korean and Indian governments.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Suro of Geumgwan Gaya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the 1st century CE the Byeonhan statelets developed into the Gaya confederacy, traditionally dated to 42 when <b>King Suro</b> founded Geumgwan Gaya at Gimhae.</blockquote><a href="/state/byeonhan">Byeonhan</a></li><li><blockquote>Its founding legend names Ijinashi as first king, brother of <b>Suro of Geumgwan Gaya</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/daegaya">Daegaya</a></li><li><blockquote>Tradition dates its founding to 42 CE, when six golden eggs descended from heaven at Gimhae and hatched the founders of six Gaya states, the eldest being <b>Suro of Geumgwan Gaya</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/gaya-confederacy">Gaya confederacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its founding legend tells of <b>King Suro</b> hatching from a golden egg in 42 CE and marrying Heo Hwang-ok, a princess said to have sailed from a distant land called Ayuta.</blockquote><a href="/state/geumgwan-gaya">Geumgwan Gaya</a></li></ul></section>
